In your code:

if (visited[i]) continue;


visited[i] is a boolean flag that tells us whether we have already counted this element before.

continue means: skip this iteration of the loop and move to the next i.

So:

visited[i] == false → not counted yet → we proceed to count its frequency.

visited[i] == true → already counted → we skip it (do not count again).

Why do we need this?

Look at your array:

arr = {2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 3}


First time we see 2 at index 0 → count it → mark all other 2’s as visited (visited[2] = true).

Next time in the outer loop when i = 2 → arr[2] = 2 → but visited[2] = true → we skip, because we already counted 2.

Without this check, we would count the same element multiple times, which is wrong.

So in short:

visited[i] == true → "I already counted this number somewhere earlier, no need to count again."
visited[i] == false → "I haven't counted this number yet, proceed."

