Brute Force :

import java.util.*;
public class Main{
    public static int FindFrequency(int arr[] , int n){
        int count = 0;
        for(int x:arr){
            if(arr[x]==n){
                count++;
            }   
        }
        
        return count;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args){
        int[] arr = {2, 1, 2, 3, 4, 3};
        int n = 10;
        int result = FindFrequency(arr , n);
        System.out.print(result);
    }
}

This approach is brute force because for every query, you scan the entire array again.
If array size = N and queries = Q, time complexity = O(N × Q).
Line-by-line

for (int v : arr) iterates array once.

if (v == x) increments cnt.

Complexity

One call: O(N) where N = arr.length.

With Q queries: O(Q * N).
Example: if N = 10^5 and Q = 10^5, operations ≈ 10^10. If CPU ~ 10^8 ops/sec → 10^10 / 10^8 = 100 seconds (too slow).

